Licence for the derived dataset (proteins.json)
===============================================

proteins.json is a derived work built from the five sources below by the
scripts in pipeline/. It is released under

    Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

CC BY 4.0 is the most restrictive of the inbound terms (it comes from
UniProt), so applying it to the whole derived file keeps every upstream
obligation satisfied. The repository's MIT licence covers the code only.

If you reuse this file: credit the sources below, link to the CC BY licence,
and state that the data has been modified — it has. Columns are bucketed,
renamed, filtered and joined; nothing here is a verbatim copy of any source.


Upstream sources
----------------

UniProt — reviewed human proteome
    Protein names, sequence length, mass, subcellular location, keywords,
    EC numbers, disease annotations, gene synonyms.
    Licence: CC BY 4.0 — https://www.uniprot.org/help/license
    Cite: The UniProt Consortium. UniProt: the Universal Protein
    Knowledgebase in 2025. Nucleic Acids Res 53:D609-D617 (2025).
    doi:10.1093/nar/gkae1010
    THIS IS THE SOURCE THAT MAKES THE DERIVED FILE CC BY. Attribution is a
    licence condition here, not a courtesy.

Reactome — pathway membership and hierarchy
    Licence: CC0 1.0 for annotation and identifier-mapping files, which is
    what is used here. (Reactome moved from CC BY 4.0 to CC0 for these
    files in December 2017; pathway diagrams and software remain CC BY 4.0.)
    https://reactome.org/license
    Cite: Ragueneau E, Gong C, Sinquin P, et al. The Reactome Knowledgebase
    2026. Nucleic Acids Res (2025). doi:10.1093/nar/gkaf1223

HGNC — chromosomal locations
    Licence: CC0 — https://www.genenames.org/about/license/
    Cite: Seal RL, Braschi B, Gray K, McClay J, Tweedie S, Bruford EA.
    Genenames.org: the HGNC and PGNC resources in 2026. Nucleic Acids Res
    54(D1):D1098-D1107. doi:10.1093/nar/gkaf1229

NCBI Gene — gene2pubmed, Homo_sapiens.gene_info
    Publication counts (used to rank proteins by how well known they are)
    and gene aliases.
    US Government public domain; NCBI places no restrictions on use or
    redistribution. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/
    Acknowledgement requested: Source: National Library of Medicine.

Gene-Ages — conservation depth
    Consensus gene age across 13 orthology databases.
    Licence: MIT, Copyright (c) 2016 Benjamin J. Liebeskind.
    https://github.com/marcottelab/Gene-Ages
    Cite: Liebeskind BJ, McWhite CD, Marcotte EM. Towards Consensus Gene
    Ages. Genome Biology and Evolution 8(6):1812-1823 (2016).
    doi:10.1093/gbe/evw113

